<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Debug-only manifest overlay.

  Two things the instrumented tests need that the shipped app must not have:

  1. A network security config, so the tests can reach a local http://localhost
     MockWebServer. The release manifest keeps the platform default (cleartext
     refused).

  2. android.permission.INTERNET.

     This is not a relaxation of the privacy promise — it is a correction of a
     wrong assumption. Android gates *every* socket on INTERNET, including a
     loopback connection to 127.0.0.1. When Stage 7b removed INTERNET from the
     main manifest (correctly: the shipped app must not reach the network), the
     two MockWebServer device suites began failing in setUp with
     "lateinit property transport has not been initialized", because
     OkHttpTtsTransport could not open its socket. 17 tests failed for a reason
     that had nothing to do with the behaviour they assert.

     The permission is declared here, in the debug source set only. It is not in
     app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml, so it is not in the release APK, and
     ManifestAudit / regression test R11 — which read the main manifest — still
     fail the build if it ever appears there.

     Verified: the release APK built from this tree declares no INTERNET
     permission. See SONATA_PRIVACY_AND_SECURITY.md.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <application android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config" />
</manifest>
